1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,904
Two words in the Qur’an.

2
00:00:01,904 --> 00:00:03,407
Both translated as rain.

3
00:00:03,407 --> 00:00:05,810
So, what’s the difference?

4
00:00:05,810 --> 00:00:07,140
Matar means rain.

5
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:09,291
In the Qur’an, it often appears

6
00:00:09,291 --> 00:00:11,610
in harmful contexts.

7
00:00:11,610 --> 00:00:13,580
In 7:84, it describes

8
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:15,077
a destructive shower,

9
00:00:15,077 --> 00:00:17,473
not normal rainfall.

10
00:00:17,473 --> 00:00:19,331
Ghayth highlights welcome rain,

11
00:00:19,331 --> 00:00:23,073
bringing relief.

12
00:00:23,073 --> 00:00:25,233
In 42:28, Allah sends rain

13
00:00:25,233 --> 00:00:26,950
after people lose hope,

14
00:00:26,950 --> 00:00:29,873
and spreads His mercy.

15
00:00:29,873 --> 00:00:32,250
Matar doesn’t always mean punishment.

16
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:34,527
4:102 uses it for rain

17
00:00:34,527 --> 00:00:36,673
causing hardship.

18
00:00:36,673 --> 00:00:39,123
Matar: often harmful contexts.

19
00:00:39,123 --> 00:00:41,473
Ghayth: rain as relief.

20
00:00:41,473 --> 00:00:44,723
Always read the context.
